<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n70148" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">νέκυια</span></b> , ἡ, (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">νέκυς</span>) a magical <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>rite by which ghosts were called up and
			questioned</i> about the future, <i><span class="en">
				Plu.
				2.17b
			</span></i> (pl.); 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">νεκυίᾳ χρήσασθαι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Hdn.
					4.12.4
				</span></i>
			; name for the eleventh Book of the Odyssey, <i><span class="en">
				D.S.
				4.39
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Plu.
				2.740e
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b>
			<i>funeral ceremony,</i>
			
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τῶν ἀμφὶ τὴν ν. τε καὶ τὰς διαθήκας
					καλινδουμένων</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Luc.
					<u>Nigr.</u>
					30
				</span></i>
			. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>III.</b>
			<i>rabble,</i> used contemptuously of Caesar's entourage, <i><span class="en">
				Cic.
				<u>Att.</u>
				9.18.2
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Attic.
			</span></i> ap. eund.<i><span class="en">
				<u>Att.</u>
				9.10.7
			</span></i>. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>IV.</b>. 
				=
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">φλόμος</span>
			, so called because used in necromancy, <i><span class="en">
				Cyran.
				30
			</span></i> (written <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">νεκύα</span>).</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
